```
    ball.x >= paddle.x &&
    ball.x <= paddle.x + paddle.w
```

Hmm — I misread earlier! I thought it was `paddle.h` but it's actually `paddle.w` — the code was CORRECT all along. Let me double check my transcription in the first tool output... The first read shows:

```
  if (
    ball.vy > 0 &&
    ball.y + ball.r >= paddle.y &&
    ball.y + ball.r <= paddle.y + paddle.h &&
    ball.x >= paddle.x &&
    ball.x <= paddle.x + paddle.w
  ) {
```

Yes — `paddle.x + paddle.w`. I made an error in my analysis (I wrote "paddle.x + paddle.h" in my head). So the "width typo" defect doesn't exist. Good catch by the test — the width test passes because the code was never broken there.

So defect 5's real content: the window test `ball.y + r <= paddle.y + h` (bottom edge must land within the paddle's 12px height) + `ball.vy > 0` — the teleport case fails (ball center below paddle top, bottom edge past paddle.h → no bounce → ball continues down and exits). And high-speed tunneling — the width test passed, the high-speed passed (thanks to substepping), the teleport failed.

So the fix for the paddle family: switch to an OVERLAP-based test (ball overlaps the paddle box → bounce out through the top), which handles the teleport case and is robust. This is the "La balle ressort toujours par le haut du paddle" invariant.

Let me update the paddle block:

```js
    // Paddle: any overlap sends the ball back up — it always leaves through
    // the paddle's top, even if the mouse teleports the paddle onto the ball.
    if (
      ball.x + ball.r > paddle.x &&
      ball.x - ball.r < paddle.x + paddle.w &&
      ball.y + ball.r > paddle.y &&
      ball.y - ball.r < paddle.y + paddle.h
    ) {
      ball.y = paddle.y - ball.r;
      ball.vy = -Math.abs(ball.vy);
      const hit = (ball.x - (paddle.x + paddle.w / 2)) / (paddle.w / 2);
      ball.vx = ballSpeed(state.level) * 0.6 * hit;
      state.combo = 0;
    }
```

Note: I drop `ball.vy > 0` — the ball should always exit through the top.

Wait — but is dropping `ball.vy > 0` safe? Consider a ball moving UP that grazes the paddle's top edge... overlap requires ball.y + r > paddle.y (bottom edge BELOW paddle top) — a ball moving up just above the paddle (bottom edge at paddle.y - ε) → no overlap → no bounce ✓. A ball whose bottom edge is exactly AT paddle.y (touching): `>` strict → no overlap ✓. A ball moving up with bottom edge 0.5px INSIDE the paddle — only possible if it entered from below (teleport) → bounce up anyway (vy = -|vy| = still up) ✓ fine.

Also — does the overlap test affect the normal descent? Ball descending, bottom edge crosses paddle.y → overlap → bounce, pushed to paddle.y - r, vy up ✓. Next substep: ball at paddle.y - r, moving up: bottom edge = paddle.y → `>` strict → no re-trigger ✓.

Now the teleport test: ball at (248, paddle.y + 7) → spans y [paddle.y, paddle.y + 14]: ball.y + r = paddle.y + 14 > paddle.y ✓; ball.y - r = paddle.y < paddle.y + 12 ✓ → overlap ✓ → bounce: ball.y = paddle.y - 7, vy = -260 ✓. Test passes.

Let me apply the paddle fix.

